    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d356-a1">

                <pb facs="0552" n="480" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg480"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 95.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sypher to Mr. Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-08-22"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 22,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: About two months ago, I addressed the
                    Department on the subject of the exorbitant fees charged by Spanish consuls upon
                    our line of steamers plying between New Orleans and Havana. I am in receipt of a
                    letter from the managers of the company, under date of 17th, from which I
                    extract the following:</p>
                <p>“We cleared one of our steamers for Havana to-day, and the Spanish consul exacted
                    ten cents a ton on all the cargo carried by the vessel as a portion of his fees.
                    This is a very heavy tax, and we are sure none such is imposed on Spanish
                    steamers by the American authorities.”</p>
                <p>If there is any relief against such imposition, I respectfully invoke it through
                    your Department.</p>
                <p>Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. H. SYPHER.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
